## 1. Overview of State-Based Language Models (SBLMs)

State-Based Language Models (SBLMs) represent a paradigm shift from traditional transformer architectures by replacing self-attention with a state space model (SSM) that maintains a fixed-size recurrent hidden state. This design choice addresses two critical limitations of transformers: the quadratic computational complexity of self-attention and the challenge of modeling long-range dependencies efficiently. The Mamba architecture, introduced by Gu and Dao (2023), exemplifies this approach by employing a selective SSM layer that enables linear-time inference and significant throughput improvements over transformers. The SSM formalism accumulates context implicitly through a dynamical system, avoiding the explicit pairwise attention computation and thereby reducing memory and compute demands.

The Mamba-2 variant further refines this approach with a gated SSM mechanism inspired by gated attention units, which improves training stability and inference efficiency. These innovations allow SBLMs to match or exceed transformer performance on language modeling tasks while accelerating training via work-efficient parallel scan algorithms. The state space representation also facilitates better handling of long sequences, enabling context windows exceeding 1 million tokens in some hybrid models.

Key theoretical advancements include the integration of SSM layers with transformer components and mixture-of-experts (MoE) modules, which collectively enhance model capacity and inference speed. This hybrid approach is now a dominant trend, with models like Nemotron and IBM Granite 4.0 demonstrating that combining Mamba with transformers and MoE layers can yield state-of-the-art results on diverse benchmarks.

