## 2. Mamba-Type Models

### Architectural Innovations

Mamba-2 is the foundational model introducing a selective state-space layer that replaces self-attention with a recurrent hidden state mechanism. This architecture achieves linear-time inference complexity and significant throughput gains compared to transformers. The incorporation of a SiLU-based gating mechanism improves training dynamics and inference speed, making it a cornerstone for subsequent hybrid models.

Falcon Mamba 7B is a pure Mamba-based model trained on 5.8 trillion tokens with 7.27 billion parameters, supporting arbitrary sequence lengths during inference. It outperforms many hybrid models on key benchmarks such as MMLU (Massive Multitask Language Understanding), GSM8K (math reasoning), ARC (AI2 Reasoning Challenge), and MuSR (Multimodal Understanding and Reasoning). Its architecture is optimized for long-context understanding and efficient inference.

Hybrid models like Jamba, Samba, and Zamba combine Mamba layers with transformer attention and MoE layers to balance long-context reasoning and computational efficiency. Jamba, for instance, interleaves transformer, Mamba, and MoE layers to achieve high accuracy and throughput, while Samba scales to over 1 million token context lengths by integrating global and local attention mechanisms with Mamba’s state space layers.

### Performance Benchmarks

Mamba-2 and its derivatives consistently demonstrate superior inference speed and memory efficiency compared to transformers. Falcon Mamba 7B achieves state-of-the-art results on multiple long-context benchmarks, validating the efficacy of pure Mamba architectures. Hybrid models further improve inference throughput by combining Mamba’s efficiency with transformer and MoE layers, reducing inference costs by up to 5x in some configurations.

